From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
Date: Sat, 6 Sep 2026 15:50:00 +0300
Subject: [PATCH 34/34] drm/msm/dpu: Restore bandwidth and core clock on runtime
 resume

dpu_runtime_suspend() drops the interconnect vote to zero, and the crtc disable
that precedes it drops the core clock as well: dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() takes
its !crtc->enabled branch, which memsets the perf params and then runs both
_dpu_core_perf_crtc_update_bus() and dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, 0).

dpu_runtime_resume() restores neither. It enables the clocks, reinitialises VBIF
memtypes and resumes the encoders -- which restarts scanout -- with no bandwidth
vote and the core clock at its lowest OPP. The only code that raises either again
is dpu_core_perf_crtc_update(), whose callers are all atomic commit paths
(dpu_crtc_atomic_flush, dpu_crtc_complete_commit, dpu_crtc_disable).

So between scanout restarting and userspace's first commit after resume, the DPU
runs underprovisioned. On an X1E80100 laptop (2944x1840 at 90 Hz, 522 MHz pixel
clock, one DMA pipe feeding both mixers) roughly half of all resumes produced
exactly six underruns on INTF_5, spaced 11.1 ms apart, which is the 90 Hz frame
period:

  [  173.571680] msm pm: complete enter (restoring display)
  [  174.019458] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=1
  [  174.030587] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=2
  [  174.041688] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=3
  [  174.052778] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=4
  [  174.063899] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=5
  [  174.075090] msm dpu: underrun intf=6 count=6

The mixers paint INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR while starved -- dpu_encoder_phys_vid sets
underflow_clr to 0xff -- so this is a brief full-screen blue flash. No IOMMU
faults accompany it and the source addresses read back correctly, so the pipes
are starved rather than blocked. It is not duration dependent: a 7 s suspend
reproduced it and 3 minute ones came back clean.

Restoring only the bandwidth vote was measured and is not sufficient. With
avg=1220935 peak=800000 re-voted 352 us into the resume, all six underruns still
occurred, so the core clock is restored too -- after the vote, matching the
ordering dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() documents ("bandwidth is available before
clock rate is increased").

Only non-zero values are remembered, so neither the zero written by the crtc
disable nor the transient avg=0 vote seen just after resume becomes the value
restored.

Measured on an X1E80100 laptop: 4 of 8 resumes underran before, 0 of 7 after,
across suspends from 4 s to 5m37s. Logging used to establish this is not
included here.

Signed-off-by: Waltteri Hooli <wahooli@localhost>
---
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.h
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.h
@@ -101,6 +101,11 @@
 	atomic_t bandwidth_ref;
 	struct icc_path *path[2];
 	u32 num_paths;
+
+	/* last real vote/rate, so runtime resume can restore them */
+	u32 avg_bw;
+	u32 peak_bw;
+	u64 core_clk_rate;
 };
 
 struct vsync_info {
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_kms.c
@@ -1449,6 +1449,7 @@
 static int __maybe_unused dpu_runtime_resume(struct device *dev)
 {
 	int rc = -1;
+	int i;
 	struct platform_device *pdev = to_platform_device(dev);
 	struct msm_drm_private *priv = platform_get_drvdata(pdev);
 	struct dpu_kms *dpu_kms = to_dpu_kms(priv->kms);
@@ -1463,6 +1464,31 @@
 		return rc;
 	}
 
+	/*
+	 * dpu_runtime_suspend() dropped the interconnect vote to zero, and
+	 * nothing votes again until the next atomic commit -- but scanout
+	 * restarts below. Restore the last vote first, or the pipes starve for
+	 * the frames in between and the mixers paint INTF_UNDERFLOW_COLOR.
+	 */
+	for (i = 0; i < dpu_kms->num_paths; i++)
+		icc_set_bw(dpu_kms->path[i], dpu_kms->avg_bw, dpu_kms->peak_bw);
+
+	/*
+	 * Same gap for the core clock: the crtc disable that precedes suspend
+	 * runs dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, 0), and nothing raises it again until
+	 * the next commit. Restore it after the bw vote, matching the ordering
+	 * dpu_core_perf_crtc_update() documents.
+	 */
+	if (dpu_kms->core_clk_rate) {
+		int crc = dev_pm_opp_set_rate(dev, dpu_kms->core_clk_rate);
+
+		if (crc)
+			DPU_ERROR("failed to restore core clock rate %llu: %d\n",
+				  dpu_kms->core_clk_rate, crc);
+		else
+			dpu_kms->perf.core_clk_rate = dpu_kms->core_clk_rate;
+	}
+
 	dpu_vbif_init_memtypes(dpu_kms);
 
 	drm_for_each_encoder(encoder, ddev)
--- a/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c
+++ b/drivers/gpu/drm/msm/disp/dpu1/dpu_core_perf.c
@@ -243,6 +243,16 @@
 
 	avg_bw /= kms->num_paths;
 
+	/*
+	 * Only remember a real vote. Disabling a crtc memsets its perf params
+	 * and votes zero, and that happens before runtime suspend -- recording
+	 * it would make the restore below a no-op.
+	 */
+	if (avg_bw) {
+		kms->avg_bw = avg_bw;
+		kms->peak_bw = peak_bw;
+	}
+
 	for (i = 0; i < kms->num_paths; i++)
 		icc_set_bw(kms->path[i], avg_bw, peak_bw);
 
@@ -402,6 +412,9 @@
 		}
 
 		kms->perf.core_clk_rate = clk_rate;
+		/* Same reasoning as the bw vote: disable sets this to 0. */
+		if (clk_rate)
+			kms->core_clk_rate = clk_rate;
 		DRM_DEBUG_ATOMIC("update clk rate = %lld HZ\n", clk_rate);
 	}
 	return 0;
